{"kind":"request","text":"AR-4 needs a design ruling before a head. Two blockers in the conditions as written (read at main d910468d):\n\n(1) Production cannot tell \"no destination\" from \"refused\". The Directory host answers {ok:false, reason:\"forbidden\"} for every case of one combined check: archive slot not \"journal\", register or claim mismatch, and host or namespace mismatch (packages/scope/src/archive-host-item.ts:35-37). The adapter maps a missing row to forbidden too (apps/room/archive.ts:74). archive-target.ts:139 ('archive !== \"journal\"') never sees a row in production. Every established Directory has the repository row (directory.ts establish); an unbound room has an archive slot that is not \"journal\", set later by bind-archive (archive-binding.ts:29-32).\n\n(2) Clearing the wake makes things worse. With next null and pending false, signal() (archive-row.ts:502-511) re-arms on every commit. Room A commits every minute, so it would pass about 60 times an hour instead of once. Today the pending retry is what keeps it quiet. A disabled pass that the next commit does not undo needs a durable marker, and the wake record accepts only capacityDeferrals, next, pending, retries and stopped (archive-row.ts:383). The in-memory #disabled (archive-driver.ts:128) is lost on eviction.\n\nAlso, nothing wakes the siblings when the Directory binds. Rules, Site and lanes wake only on their own commits or on constructor activation (object.ts:369-378).\n\nOptions:\n(A) New answer plus marker. archiveHostItem answers a distinct reason 'unbound' when the row is present and the archive slot is not \"journal\"; the other checks stay forbidden. resolve returns a sentinel, and the pass ends 'disabled' with a durable marker (a new 'unbound' wake field) and a daily recheck. signal() leaves the marker alone in lane, Site and Rules scopes. In the Directory, any commit clears it (Directory commits are rare; bind-archive is one), so binding archives the Directory at once and its siblings at their next daily recheck. Transient failures (throws, timeouts, unreadable replies, branch not ready, mismatches) keep the AR-1 backoff. Cost on grow-demo: about 98 requests per hour falls to 4 passes a day, roughly 100 requests a day. Wake floor: 24 h per unbound scope.\n(B) Smaller. Every target-missing result backs off to a 24 h cap instead of 1 h, and a Directory commit clears its own pending retry. There is no new answer shape, but a transient null (forbidden for a real mismatch, a birth read returning null) also waits 24 h. That breaks condition 3.\n(C) As (A), plus a successful Directory pass poking its siblings through a new archiveWake RPC, so binding takes effect at once everywhere. This adds a cross-scope wake path and needs a security review.\n\nI recommend A: it meets conditions 1, 3 and 5, and condition 2 for the Directory at once and for the siblings within 24 h without a deployment. C can follow if same-day archiving after a bind matters. Not kernel: no rules or definition change; the archive host answer and the wake record are runtime. Which do you rule?","body":{"conditions":"A ruling of A, B or C for AR-4, or a revised condition 2.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}